One binding, one block: `:focus { color: blue }` written first,
`:hover { color: red }` written second. The two rules have equal
specificity ((0,2,0) each), style the same element and set the same
property, so they compete and only the sheet position decides. The block's
rule list (its `[@@@css.blocks]` entry, which the ppx writes) gives generate a block edge
"focus before hover", so the sheet follows the block's source order, as
plain CSS reads it, whatever the definition order of the two rules:

  $ cat > single_block.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ("._a_1hoverclr:hover{color:red;}", "_a_1hoverclr", "0,2,0 own", "color", 1, false)]
  > [@@@css ("._a_2focusclr:focus{color:blue;}", "_a_2focusclr", "0,2,0 own", "color", 1, false)]
  > [@@@css.blocks [(3, "", [1; 0])]]
  > EOF

  $ styled-ppx.generate single_block.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  ._a_2focusclr:focus{color:blue;}
  ._a_1hoverclr:hover{color:red;}

For an element that is both hovered and focused, `color: red` (hover, written
last) wins. Two blocks that write the same pair in opposite orders are a
conflict, see `block-order-pseudo-class.t`.
